# SECTION 2: WHAT THE STREAM COSTS, measured on this run rather than on the storm fixtures

  152068 bytes, 405 R lines, cycles 81 to 8735
  375.5 bytes per retired record;  17.57 bytes per cycle

A CORRECTION TO MY OWN EARLIER FIGURE, recorded here because it travelled to the DV Lead before it was checked.
I first sized the export by CYCLES at 77.3 bytes each, a rate taken from the six retained files under
probe_t150_979350a. Those are storm fixtures whose purpose is to fire every event source, and they are not
representative of a program run: this run writes 17.6 bytes per cycle against their 69 to 109. Bytes per RECORD
is the stable quantity, 302 to 531 across all seven files against a factor of 6.2 for bytes per cycle, because
the R lines scale with the program and the E lines with bus activity that a storm maximises.

  Re-derived per scope over round 2's 56 planned runs, each entry sized by its own mean retired-record count
  from round 1, and the irq entry by the 35 wave runs that report one:

    scope                                    runs   records    export at 375 to 531 B/record
    irq family (the landed scope)               3    11,373    4.3 to 6.0 MB
    every measured entry                       45   390,167     147 to 207 MB
    every fire-check entry (gen_test_)         54   405,925     152 to 216 MB

  Round 1's whole out-tree is 319 MB. A purpose-4 round already prunes the export from every PASS and RED-OK
  run unless the entry sets keep_artifacts, so these are PEAK figures during a round, not what survives it.

