# SECTION 4: the retention rule sees the default

prune_plan and the run's argv read ONE predicate, gen_flow_util.export_file_for, so a defaulted export is
pruned by exactly the rule that prunes a named one. The self-test above proves it on a fabricated entry that
names no plusarg and carries the default feature group: it is planned for pruning with the default file name,
and a non-default entry beside it is not. The case uses its own testlist so the counts the existing retention
cases assert are not perturbed.

================================================================
# SECTION 5: the default does not refuse the entry when it is promoted to measured
# stamp 2026-09-05T12:38:33Z

The default adds a plusarg to the list measured_refusal judges (gen_run.py:53 and :248 read one expression), so a
promotion of the irq entry to measured could have been refused by a gate that never saw that knob before. Checked
rather than assumed, on the real testlist entry with measured forced true:

  effective plusargs  ['+gen_fetch_en_at_reset=0', '+gen_knob_irq_line_mix=single', '+gen_export_file=gen_export.txt']
  measured_refusal    None
  export_origin       default
  gen_export_file in debug_only_plusargs?  False
  MEASURED_KNOB_CONDITIONS triggers  ['gen_knob_icache_ecc_err_rate', 'gen_knob_icache_data_ecc_err_rate']

Neither gate engages: the export knob is not debug-only, and both LOG-077 rows trigger on the icache ECC rate
knobs. The entry is measured false today; this is the check that the default will not block the promotion the
round-2 scope has for it.

ONE GAP NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT. The loader's containment rule, that an export file is a plain name inside the run
directory, is applied to plusargs an ENTRY lists, and a defaulted export never passes through it. The default value
is a constant, so containment holds by construction, and the self-test asserts that constant is non-empty, not
absolute and free of "..". A future scope that computed a file name per entry would have to route it through the
loader's rule instead.

